  {
    "id": "gate-programming-and-data-structures-test-1-q007",
    "exam": {
      "name": "GATE",
      "fullName": "Graduate Aptitude Test in Engineering",
      "year": null,
      "session": "Programming and Data Structures"
    },
    "question": {
      "pdfName": "Computer_Organization_and_Architecture_2.pdf",
      "direction": "Consider a 5 - stage pipeline:\n1. Instruction fetch           2. Register Read\n3. ALU operation               4. Data Access\n5. Register write\nThe time taken for memory access is 200 ns, CPU operation is 200 ns, register read or write is 100 ns. Consider a program with the following kind of instructions:\nI1: Load word\nI2: Store word\nI3: ALU operation\nI4: BEQ (Branch if equal)",
      "text": "If each instruction takes exactly one clock cycle, under ideal conditions, what is the time between I1 and I4 with out pipelining?",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "800 ns"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "2100 ns"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "2400 ns"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "600 ns"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "C",
      "correctText": "2400 ns",
      "explanation": "Without using pipelining the time taken for a single instruction is the sum of the times required by all the stages of the pipeline.\ni.e., IF + RR + ALU + DA + RW\n= 200 + 100 + 200 + 200 + 100 = 800ns\nEach instruction requires one clock cycle.\nTime between I4 and I1 is 800 ns * 3(instructions)\n= 2400 ns.                                    Choice (C)",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
    "classification": {
      "subject": "Programming and Data Structures",
      "topic": "Pipelining",
      "subtopic": "Pipeline timing",
      "tags": []
    },
    "source": {
