    "question": {
      "pdfName": "Computer_Organization_and_Architecture_2.pdf",
      "direction": "Directions for questions 1 to 25: Select the correct alternative from the given choices.",
      "text": "A process has five interrupt lines, numbered 0 – 4 and a policy that low numbered interrupts have priority over higher numbered ones. The processor starts with no interrupts pending and the following sequence of interrupts occurs: 4, 3, 0, 1, 2, 3\nAssume that handling any interrupt takes enough time that two more interrupts arrive while the first interrupt is being handled, until all of the interrupts have arrived and that interrupts cannot interrupt each other.\nIn what order the interrupts are handled ?",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "0, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "4, 3, 0, 1, 2, 3"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "4, 0, 1, 3, 2, 3"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "4, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "D",
      "correctText": "4, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3",
      "explanation": "Lower numbered interrupts have high priority.\nLet initially 4 is handled, mean while, 3 and 0 will occur. Give priority to 0.\nWhile 0 is handled, 1 and 2 will occur with 3 also in pending Queue. Next handle 1, 2, 3, 3.\n∴ Order is 4, 0, 1, 2, 3, 3.              Choice (D)",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
    "classification": {
      "subject": "Programming and Data Structures",
      "topic": "I/O and Interrupts",
      "subtopic": "Interrupt priority",
      "tags": []
    },
    "source": {
